    "turn_text": "Well, I learned that in an individual like Doug Wilder who was as unique in individuals that anybody had met in politics. I'm proud to call him a mentor. I call him a friend. I also know he's been someone who, anyone who works with Doug Wilder has ups and downs and I've had my series of ups and downs with him. Doug felt that his background and experience was more than sufficient. He was better qualified than any other candidate and went about running back in 1989 when a book had been written, you know, Helle Fries over first and, you know, Helle didn't freeze over. He got elected lieutenant governor. He got elected governor. There were many rooms many times and that campaign where I had the fee, you know, I always might be the only white guy in the room, which was the opposite of what I'd grown up with, and frankly, probably opposite of what many African-Americans being in politics were growing up with, where they had been the, maybe the only African-American individual in the room. Politics is always a bit below the surface. I learned, particularly from Doug and I tried to convey this when I had a few opportunities to get to know Barack Obama that oftentimes for the first The challenge oftentimes the African American candidate has to be the first that",
